# Diagnostics over changing projections

A hover answers a question asked at one moment. A diagnostic may arrive later,
without a cursor and sometimes without a request that the editor can directly see.
Meanwhile the author may add prose, edit a fragment, change a template, or close a
session. Keeping a warning on screen is therefore not only a mapping problem; it is
an ownership and freshness problem.

Literate gives generated-language problems their own editor collection. They do not
replace diagnostics about fragment syntax or project generation. The collection is
assembled from active language sessions, and each session retains independent
reports for the projected outputs it owns.

## A numbered diagnostic needs three matching identities

A warning remains trustworthy only if it came from the same running tool, describes
the same generated draft, and still belongs to the source the author is viewing.
In protocol terms, a numbered report is provably current only when three forms of
identity still agree:

1. **Session identity:** it came from the same client process that owns the current
   synchronized documents.
2. **Wire identity:** it describes the monotonically increasing document revision
   currently associated with that projected URI.
3. **Snapshot freshness:** the captured source, templates, workspace, trust, and
   diagnostic setting still satisfy the session's freshness check.

A matching number from a replacement process is not enough. Neither is a live
process whose source snapshot has been invalidated. These conditions prevent an old
answer from acquiring authority merely because some later state looks similar.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Report[Incoming diagnostic report] --> Session{Current client session?}
    Session -->|No| Drop[Omit report]
    Session -->|Yes| Revision{Current projected document revision?}
    Revision -->|No| Drop
    Revision -->|Yes| Fresh{Captured inputs still fresh?}
    Fresh -->|No| Drop
    Fresh -->|Yes| Map[Map generated ranges]
    Map --> Publish[Publish owned source problems]
```

The arrows describe the provable path for a report carrying a document revision,
not protocol traffic. Mapping happens only after identity and freshness are
established. An unversioned push report cannot pass the revision proof; its weaker,
best-effort handling is described below.

## Pull reports take precedence over push reports

Servers deliver diagnostics in two broad ways. A push-capable server sends a
notification when it chooses. A pull-capable server lets the client request a report
for a document. When pull diagnostics are advertised, Literate uses them and ignores
push reports for that session.

Pull requests run asynchronously after synchronization. Refresh notifications
coalesce into one bounded, sequential pass rather than starting overlapping cycles.
There is no polling loop and no automatic retry storm. Literate requests complete
reports without presenting a previous result identifier. A complete report replaces
that output's owned problems; other report forms and related-document payloads do
not widen ownership.

The pull operation carries cancellation and freshness state. Invalidation, session
replacement, or disposal aborts pending requests. A late completion is checked again
before it can publish anything.

## Push reports expose an unavoidable ambiguity

Literate subscribes to push notifications before synchronizing documents, because a
fast server may report immediately after seeing an open or change message. A push
that includes the exact current wire revision can be staged during synchronization
and considered once synchronization finishes.

A push without a document revision cannot be correlated as strongly. During
synchronization it is ignored. After synchronization, it is accepted only while the
session is fresh, idle, and owns the URI. Even then freshness is **best effort**: the
protocol provides no evidence that a late unnumbered report describes the newest
text rather than an earlier text.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Owner as Diagnostics owner
    participant Server
    participant Problems as Problems collection

    Owner->>Server: Synchronize projected document at revision r
    Server-->>Owner: Numbered report for r
    Owner->>Owner: Confirm session, r, and snapshot freshness
    Owner->>Problems: Publish mapped ranges
    Owner->>Server: Synchronize changed text at revision r plus one
    Server-->>Owner: Late report for r
    Owner->>Owner: Reject mismatched report
```

This sequence shows the provable case. If the late report carried no revision, the
last rejection could not be justified from protocol data alone. Literate documents
that limitation instead of pretending that a timestamp or arrival order proves
causality.

## Invalidation clears first

When source input becomes stale, visible language problems are cleared immediately
and pending diagnostic work is aborted. The editor must not continue showing a
warning against text that no longer owns it while a new snapshot is being captured.

An identical snapshot is different. If projected documents, mappings, and wire
revisions are unchanged, the diagnostics owner updates its freshness callback
without clearing visible problems, repeating synchronization, or cancelling an
active pull. This avoids flicker and unnecessary work when an event changes no
semantic input.

Disabling language diagnostics for a resource also clears that session's problems.
Interactive services may still synchronize projections for their own requests; the
setting controls diagnostic ownership, not every use of the language server.

## Cached reports survive only unchanged projected text

A prose-only edit can shift a fragment down several source lines without changing
the generated output. The server need not receive new projected text, so its wire
revision remains unchanged. A previously accepted report may still describe the
right generated range, but its old source location must be recalculated.

Literate keeps a bounded sanitized cache keyed by client identity, generated URI,
and wire revision. After a new source snapshot is captured, a cached report is
reused only if that same client still gives the URI the same revision. The raw
generated range is then mapped through the new projection, moving the visible
problem to the fragment's new source line.

If generated text changes, synchronization advances the wire revision and the cache
is discarded. Returning later to identical text does not revive an older report,
because wire revisions do not repeat. Stopping a client purges its cache even when
no active source session remains.

The cache stores only bounded, sanitized ranges and metadata. It does not retain a
whole discarded source snapshot, server-private data, related locations, or
executable links.

## Diagnostic ranges may split at seams

A compiler error can cover an expression assembled from several fragments. Unlike a
navigation command, a diagnostic can usefully highlight every literal source
intersection. Literate therefore splits a generated range at projection seams and
returns bounded, deduplicated source ranges.

This still does not authorize invention:

- template-wrapper-only ranges are omitted;
- inserted indentation and reference markers receive no source range;
- intervening prose is never enclosed to make two fragments look contiguous;
- repeated expansions and repeated template slots that map to the same source range
  are deduplicated;
- malformed or out-of-bounds ranges are omitted rather than clamped.

The mapper may retain bounded message text, severity, code, source label, and the
standard unnecessary or deprecated tags. Related information and code-description
links are not copied. An unsafe auxiliary location must not smuggle navigation or
command behavior into the Problems collection.

## Reports keep independent ownership

Several outputs or sessions may report the same source problem. The diagnostics
controller merges active reports by source URI and diagnostic identity, so duplicates appear once.
Clearing one output report does not erase an equal problem still owned by another
report. Stopping one session likewise leaves problems independently owned by other
live sessions.

Counts, serialized cache size, reports per client, clients with caches, mapped ranges,
and source-file publication all have bounds. Excess server data is omitted or an
oversized cache entry is refused; limits do not turn a partial mutating operation
into success because diagnostics are observational. Lifecycle cleanup releases all
reports, cache subscriptions, and pending pulls.

Persistent diagnostics show why freshness cannot be one boolean checked only when
work starts. It is a continuing relationship among snapshot, synchronized document,
process, and reply. The next chapter applies an even stricter proof to replies that
propose changing literate source.
